  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Introduction\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU)** cache is a classic data structure that evicts the item that has not been accessed for the longest period when the cache reaches its capacity. Python's `collections.OrderedDict` provides a powerful foundation for implementing an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and allows moving keys to the end, which aligns naturally with the \"most recently used\" semantics.\n\nThis tutorial walks through building a complete `LRUCache` class, explains the behavior of `get` and `put`, analyzes edge cases, provides unit tests, and concludes with a worked example and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Core Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU (Least Recently Used) cache using OrderedDict.\n    \n    The cache maintains a fixed maximum capacity. When full, the least\n    recently accessed item is evicted upon insertion of a new item.\n    Both get() and put() update the recency order.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n                       Must be a positive integer.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n    \n    def get(self, key):\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with key.\n        \n        If the key exists, its recency is updated (moved to most recent).\n        If the key does not exist, returns None.\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n        \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or None if not present.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move key to the end to mark it as most recently used\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n    \n    def put(self, key, value):\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair.\n        \n        If the key already exists, its value is updated and its recency\n        is refreshed. If the key is new and the cache is at capacity,\n        the least recently used item (the",
